<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Bahia Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1837 CE – 1838 CE · capital Salvador</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sabinada, a revolt that proclaimed a republic in Salvador in 1837 until the young Emperor Pedro II came of age, crushed within four months.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the unstable regency period in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>, discontent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> over central control and military recruitment for the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a> came to a head. On 7 November 1837 rebels led by the doctor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-sabino-vieira">Francisco Sabino Vieira</a> took the city and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> Republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The revolt was led by urban professionals, officers and soldiers. Its leaders declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> independent only until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> reached adulthood, a compromise reflecting divisions among them. They offered freedom to Brazilian-born enslaved people who fought for the republic. The rebels never controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconcavo">Recôncavo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> region, whose planters supported the imperial government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Imperial forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> by land and sea and retook it in March 1838 after fierce street fighting with heavy loss of life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-sabino-vieira">Sabino</a> was exiled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-mato-grosso">Mato Grosso</a>, and the revolt became known as the Sabinada after him.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1837 CE – 1838 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.26%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1837 CE – 1838 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salvador</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco Sabino Vieira</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sabinada, Bahian Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=bahia-republic" aria-label="Bahia Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3624 2661 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bahia Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3944 2917L3846 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3846 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3944 2917h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Salvador. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=bahia-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
